The OpenRC boot sequence from kernel handoff to user login, including runlevel transitions and dependency resolution.

@text
Boot Sequence Overview

@code bash
# 1. Firmware (UEFI/BIOS) loads bootloader
# 2. GRUB loads kernel + initramfs
# 3. Kernel mounts root filesystem, executes /sbin/init → /sbin/openrc-init
# 4. OpenRC processes runlevels in order:
#
#    sysinit  →  boot  →  default
#
# 5. Display manager (sddm/lightdm) starts → user login
# 6. On shutdown:
#
#    shutdown  →  power off / reboot

@text
sysinit Runlevel

The earliest stage, runs before any other runlevel. Handles fundamental system setup.

@code bash
# Typical sysinit services (managed by OpenRC, rarely modified):
# devfs        — mount /dev
# dmesg        — set dmesg log level
# udev         — device manager (creates /dev nodes)
# udev-trigger — replay udev events for coldplug devices
# cgroups      — set up cgroups (v1/v2)
# sysfs        — mount /sys
# procfs       — mount /proc

# View sysinit services
ls /etc/runlevels/sysinit/
rc-status -r sysinit

@text
boot Runlevel

Essential services needed before normal operation.

@code bash
# Typical boot services:
# modules      — load kernel modules from /etc/modules-load.d/
# hwclock      — set system clock from hardware clock
# keymaps      — load keyboard layout (/etc/conf.d/keymaps)
# hostname     — set hostname
# sysctl       — apply /etc/sysctl.d/ parameters
# root         — remount root filesystem read-write
# localmount   — mount local filesystems from /etc/fstab
# swap         — activate swap
# bootmisc     — miscellaneous boot tasks (cleanup /tmp, etc.)

# View boot services
ls /etc/runlevels/boot/
rc-status -r boot

@text
default Runlevel

Normal operation — user-facing services.

@code bash
# Typical default services on an Artix desktop:
# dbus         — D-Bus message bus (required by most desktop services)
# elogind      — seat/session management (logind replacement)
# NetworkManager — network management
# syslog-ng    — system logging
# cronie       — cron daemon
# sddm         — display manager
# bluetoothd   — bluetooth daemon
# cupsd        — printing

# View default services
ls /etc/runlevels/default/
rc-status -r default

@text
shutdown Runlevel

Cleanup before power off or reboot.

@code bash
# Typical shutdown services:
# savecache    — save dependency cache
# killprocs    — terminate remaining processes
# mount-ro     — remount filesystems read-only

# View shutdown services
ls /etc/runlevels/shutdown/

@text
Dependency Resolution

OpenRC resolves service dependencies to determine start order within each runlevel.

@code bash
# Dependency types (from init script depend() blocks):
# need  → hard requirement, must succeed
# use   → soft requirement, start if available
# after → start ordering (no dependency)
# before → reverse ordering
# provide → virtual service alias

# Example dependency chain:
# sshd needs net → net needs localmount → localmount needs root
# OpenRC calculates correct start order automatically

# View dependency tree
rc-service sshd describe

# Parallel startup:
# OpenRC can start independent services in parallel
# Controlled by rc_parallel in /etc/rc.conf:
# rc_parallel="YES"    # Enable parallel boot (faster but harder to debug)
# rc_parallel="NO"     # Sequential boot (default, safer)

@text
Configuration — /etc/rc.conf

@code bash
# Key /etc/rc.conf settings:

# rc_parallel="NO"           # Parallel service startup
# rc_interactive="YES"       # Allow interactive boot (press 'i')
# rc_logger="YES"            # Log boot messages to /var/log/rc.log
# rc_log_path="/var/log/rc.log"  # Boot log location

# unicode="YES"              # Enable Unicode console support
# rc_tty_number=12           # Number of TTYs to start

# rc_cgroup_mode="hybrid"    # cgroup mode: legacy, hybrid, unified
# rc_need=""                 # Global dependencies for all services

# View current config
cat /etc/rc.conf

@text
Troubleshooting Boot

@code bash
# Boot into a minimal state (add to kernel params)
# softlevel=single          — single-user mode (rescue)
# softlevel=boot            — stop at boot runlevel
# noinit                    — drop to shell before init

# Enable boot logging
# In /etc/rc.conf: rc_logger="YES"
# Then check: /var/log/rc.log

# Interactive boot — choose services one by one
# In /etc/rc.conf: rc_interactive="YES"
# At boot, press 'i' when prompted

# Check for crashed services after boot
rc-status -c

# View boot time for each service
rc-status -a

# Manually step through boot
sudo openrc sysinit
sudo openrc boot
sudo openrc default
